\par\par \b \uc2 WEEKAHEAD-Indian rupee, bond yields to track US Treasuries in truncated week\b0\par\par\uc2 528 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 August 2023\par 03:59\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 MUMBAI, Aug 14 (Reuters) - The Indian rupee and government bond yields will largely track U.S. Treasuries in a holiday-shortened week, as traders monitor whether the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  continues to defend the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and if its forecast for retail inflation will pan out.\par \par \uc2 India's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  and money markets are off this Tuesday and Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 "We have a holiday-shortened week ahead of us. Keep a tab on U.S. (10-year) yields, which are near 4% despite pricing for further rate hikes diminishing," said Kunal Kurani, associate vice president at Mecklai Financial.\par \par \uc2 The rupee ended at 82.8450 last Friday, shedding 0.12% in a week in which it traded in a narrow 20-paisa range on likely \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \uc2 Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The \uc2 RBI\uc2  is expected to keep defending the 82.85-82.90 level.\par \par \uc2 "We expect the \uc2 RBI\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which will keep the USD/INR pair in a tight range," \uc2 ICICI Bank\uc2  said in a note.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the benchmark 7.26% 2033 bond yield ended at 7.2021% on Friday. It rose one basis point (bps) last week, after rising an aggregate of 11 bps in the previous two weeks.\par \par \uc2 Traders expect the benchmark bond yield to be in the 7.16-7.24% zone this week.\par \par \uc2 The recent climb in yields is due to the spike in U.S. yields and concerns that local retail inflation may jump sharply in the near term, which could force the \uc2 RBI\uc2  to maintain higher rates for an elevated period.\par \par \uc2 Last week, the \uc2 RBI\uc2  held rates as expected, but raised its inflation forecast for the current quarter and the fiscal year.\par \par \uc2 Retail inflation data for July is due on Monday evening, with an acceleration already factored in.\par \par \uc2 Inflation likely spiked to 6.40% in July on surging food prices as per a Reuters poll, breaching the upper end of the \uc2 RBI\uc2 's 2%-6% tolerance band.\par \par \uc2 This after climbing to 4.81% in June, snapping a four-month downward trend.\par \par \uc2 Nomura\uc2  expects the uncertainty on the magnitude and duration of the food price shock to have an impact of three to six months and once that fades, headline inflation will likely begin to converge towards core.\par \par \uc2 Puneet Pal, head of fixed income at PGIM India Mutual Fund, expects the flatness in the yield curve is likely to continue, with the 10-year bond trading in a range of 7.10% to 7.30% over the next couple of months.\par \par \uc2 KEY EVENTS:\par \par \uc2 ** India July WPI inflation - Aug 14, Monday (12:00 p.m. IST)\par \par \uc2 ** India July CPI inflation - Aug 14, Monday (5:30 p.m. IST)\par \par \uc2 ** U.S. July retail sales Aug 15, Tuesday (6:00 p.m. IST)\par \par \uc2 ** U.S. July housing starts Aug 16, Wednesday (6:00 p.m. IST)\par \par \uc2 ** U.S. July industrial production Aug 16, Wednesday (6:45 p.m. IST)\par \par \uc2 ** U.S. initial weekly jobless claims week to August. 7 - Aug 17, Thursday (10:00 a.m. IST) (Reporting by Nimesh Vora and Dharamraj Dhutia; Editing by Savio D'Souza) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2023-8-14T02:59:18.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020230814ej8e0060p\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
