\par\par \b \uc2 MORNING BID ASIA-US CPI smile fades, it's raining yen\b0\par\par\uc2 515 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 August 2023\par 23:45\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Aug 11 (Reuters) - A look at the day ahead in Asian markets from Jamie McGeever, financial markets columnist.\par \par \uc2 Markets are betting that the Fed's most aggressive rate-hiking campaign in more than 40 years is over. But now what?\par \par \uc2 Wall Street initially gave a huge thumbs up to U.S. inflation data that showed some measures of underlying price pressures cooled significantly last month, prompting rates futures markets to call an end to the Fed's tightening cycle.\par \par \uc2 But the early gains of more than 1% evaporated as longer-dated Treasury yields started to climb again, and the three main indexes closed the day flat to 0.15% higher. Asian markets, therefore, look set to open on Friday with a more subdued tone.\par \par \uc2 Investors in Asia will also have the yen on their radar - the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  slid to a 15-year low against the euro on Thursday and fell towards 145.00 per dollar, around where Japanese authorities \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  heavily last year.\par \par \uc2 The loss of bullish momentum on Wall Street on Thursday as the session progressed and renewed 'bear steepening' of the U.S. yield curve will unnerve some investors.\par \par \uc2 The short end of the bond market was a bit more stable, reflecting the view that the Fed is done raising rates. Fed fund futures pricing shows a rate hike next month is off the table completely, and only a 20% chance of another hike by year-end.\par \par \uc2 Breakeven inflation rates fell too, generally backing up that dovish view. But the long end of the Treasury curve sold off pretty aggressively once again, and rising long-term yields will do little to support risk appetite, far less boost it.\par \par \uc2 Other notable market moves on Thursday include oil. WTI and Brent crude closed down 1.7% and 1.3%, respectively, meaning U.S. crude futures now may not register a seventh straight weekly gain, which would mark the best run since May last year.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders will be on Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  alert after the yen's latest slump. The dollar is nudging 145.00 yen, around where the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  spent record yen-buying sums late last year as the yen hurtled to a 33-year low.\par \par \uc2 Elsewhere in \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2 , India's rupee had its best day in a month on Thursday, moving further away from recent all-time lows on the back of reported \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  earlier on Thursday held interest rates steady but signaled it will reduce the amount of cash in the banking system to counter inflationary pressures from high food prices.\par \par \uc2 Industrial production data from India on Friday is expected to show that output in June rose 5.0% year on year, slowing slightly from the 5.2% growth in May.\par \par \uc2 Here are key developments that could provide more direction to markets on Friday:\par \par \uc2 - Hong Kong GDP (Q2)\par \par \uc2 - India industrial production (June)\par \par \uc2 - UK GDP (Q2, prelim)\par \par \uc2 (By Jamie McGeever; editing by Deepa Babington) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2023-8-10T22:45:00.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020230810ej8a03i8h\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
