\par\par \b \uc2 INDIA RUPEE-Rupee to rise post what is seen as Fed's last rate hike\b0\par\par\uc2 444 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 Juli 2023\par 04:38\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 MUMBAI, July 27 (Reuters) - The Indian rupee is expected to appreciate slightly at open on Thursday helped by the dollar's losses after what analysts say is likely to be the last rate hike by the \uc2 U.S. Federal Reserve\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Non-deliverable forwards indicate rupee will open at around 81.92-81.94 to the U.S. dollar compared with 81.9950 on Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 The Fed hiked the policy rate by 25 basis points, in-line with expectations while leaving the door open to further rate increases. Fed Chair \uc2 Jerome Powell\uc2  at the press conference said it was "certainly possible" that the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would raise at the September meeting if the data warranted.\par \par \uc2 Analysts, however, do not reckon that there will more rate hikes.\par \par \uc2 "With two months worth of data to come before the next Fed meeting we suspect evidence of slowing inflation and softer activity won\u8217\'20\'19t make that (a rate hike) necessary," \uc2 ING Bank\uc2  said in a note.\par \par \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  pointed out that Fed will be particularly focused on the inflation data, and that it expects the next few consumer price inflation reports to be soft.\par \par \uc2 "As a result, we expect that the FOMC will skip September in order to slow the pace and will then conclude in November that inflation has slowed enough to make a final hike unnecessary," \uc2 Jan Hatzius\uc2 , chief economist at \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2 , wrote in a note to clients.\par \par \uc2 Odds of a rate hike at the September meeting were at 20%. Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were up 0.2% to 0.5% while the dollar index dropped to 100.80.\par \par \uc2 Tracking Asian peers, rupee should see "a healthy opening" after which "it can be reasonably expected it will not do much", a \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader at a bank said.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Tuesday makes short positions on USD/INR "a bit ill-founded", he said.\par \par \uc2 KEY INDICATORS: ** One-month non-deliverable rupee forward at 82.00; onshore one-month forward premium at 7.25 paisa ** USD/INR NSE August futures settled at 82.1025 on Wednesday ** USD/INR August forward premium at 9 paisa ** Dollar index down at 100.80 ** Brent crude futures up 0.9% at $83.7 per barrel ** Ten-year U.S. note yield at 3.85% ** As per NSDL data, foreign investors bought a net $349mln worth of Indian shares on Jul. 25\par \par \uc2 ** NSDL data shows foreign investors bought a net $4.8mln worth of Indian bonds on Jul. 25 (Reporting by Nimesh Vora; Editing by Nivedita Bhattacharjee) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2023-7-27T03:38:47.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020230727ej7r00b9l\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
