\par\par \b \uc2 Indian rupee's low volatility makes it an optimal carry candidate - analysts\b0\par\par\uc2 414 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 Juni 2023\par 06:02\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 MUMBAI, June 1 (Reuters) - The Indian rupee's low realized and implied volatility, thanks largely to the \uc2 Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2 's regular \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , makes it attractive for carry trades, according to analysts.\par \par \uc2 The rupee's six-month at-the-money implied volatility is down to 4.5%, the lowest since 2008. The one-year has dropped to near 5.1%, matching its multi-year lows.\par \par \uc2 "\uc2 RBI\uc2 's two-way \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .. makes rupee different from other Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . While other Asian central banks do \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 , they do not keep the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in this narrow a range," Singapore-based Khoon Goh, head of Asia research at ANZ, said.\par \par \uc2 This "most certainly" makes the rupee a preferred choice when it comes to carry trade, especially with the downside on the rupee capped by the \uc2 RBI\uc2  at the 83 level, Goh added.\par \par \uc2 A \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's volatility (vols) is an important consideration when putting on a carry trade. In a carry trade, investors look to profit off the interest rate differentials between two \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The rupee's six-month implied volatility of 4.5% is among the lowest among major Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The six-month expected volatility of the offshore Chinese yuan is 5.8%, while that of the Korean won is above 9%. For the Indonesian rupiah and the Thai baht, it is at around 7.5% and 8%, respectively.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Indian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is primarily responsible for implied vols plunging to levels not seen in more than a decade.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 RBI\uc2  has regularly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on both sides to keep the rupee in a narrow range against the dollar, preventing spillover of episodes of uncertainty in the global market.\par \par \uc2 The 30-day realized volatility on the USD/INR pair has dropped to 2.5%.\par \par \uc2 The underlying volatility of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  pair significantly impacts the eventual payoff on the carry trade. This is where the rupee has an advantage.\par \par \uc2 "The carry-to-vol ratio of the INR is amongst the best in EM (emerging markets)," \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  said in a note.\par \par \uc2 Further, it pointed out that India's improved balance-of-payment outlook is another positive for the rupee.\par \par \uc2 "Although the \uc2 RBI\uc2  is likely to cap INR appreciation, in our view, the improved \uc2 BOP\uc2  outlook is supportive of the INR, making it one of the 'good carry' markets," \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  added.\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Nimesh Vora; editing by Eileen Soreng) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2023-6-2T05:02:05.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020230602ej6200dqt\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
