\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-Indian rupee to take cues from dollar index; bond yields may rise this week\b0\par\par\uc2 580 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 Mai 2023\par 03:53\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 (Updates expected range for 10-year yield in paragraph 12)\par \par \uc2 By Dharamraj Dhutia and Sethuraman N R\par \par \uc2 MUMBAI, May 22 (Reuters) - The Indian rupee will take cues from moves in the dollar index and U.S. yields, while bonds may see some profit booking and traders will assess the impact of higher liquidity after the country's decision to withdraw highest denomination \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  note from circulation.\par \par \uc2 India's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said on Friday it will start withdrawing 2,000-rupee notes from circulation, although they will remain legal tender. The move may improve banking system liquidity, analysts and economists said.\par \par \uc2 The rupee fell 0.6% to 82.66 per dollar last week on back of a rise in the dollar index and Treasury yields.\par \par \uc2 The dollar index has surged over 2% in the past two weeks, helped by hawkish Fed officials and resilient U.S. economic data. Odds of a Fed rate hike in June have been repriced higher, propping up U.S yields.\par \par \uc2 The rupee saw a low of near-82.80 last week, a level near which most traders expect the \uc2 Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The key this week will be whether the dollar is able to maintain its recent upside trajectory and how \uc2 RBI\uc2  responds if that happens, a trader at a private sector bank said.\par \par \uc2 Jigar Trivedi, senior analyst at Reliance Securities, reckons the 83-level is critical and \uc2 RBI\uc2  will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  at that level.\par \par \uc2 Adjustments in offshore positions will also affect the rupee. Offshore bets on a rally on the Indian rupee are being stymied by the dollar's broad upward move.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, government bond yields are expected to rise as traders may look to book profits after lower-than-anticipated surplus transfer from the \uc2 RBI\uc2  to the government for the last financial year.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 RBI\uc2  board has approved a surplus transfer of 874.16 billion rupees ($10.69 billion) to the government for the fiscal year ended March 31.\par \par \uc2 The government had budgeted a dividend of 480 billion rupees for fiscal 2023 from the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , state-run banks and other financial institutions, but market participants had factored in a figure of above 1 trillion rupees.\par \par \uc2 India's benchmark bond yield stayed below the key 7% mark for most of last week, but ended at 7.0106% on Friday. Traders expect the benchmark yield to move in the 6.90% to 7.05% range this week.\par \par \uc2 Bond market sentiment improved sharply after retail inflation was below the \uc2 RBI\uc2 's upper tolerance limit for a second straight month in April, and cemented bets that rate hikes in India are completed.\par \par \uc2 "Bond yields have fallen largely after inflation eased, and we further expect the reading for May to be below 4.2%," said Yogesh Kalinge, vice president at \uc2 AK Capital Services\uc2 . KEY EVENTS: \u8226\'20\'22 U.S. May S&P Global Mfg PMI - May 23 (7:15 p.m IST) \u8226\'20\'22 U.S. April new home sales - May 23 (7:30 p.m IST)\par \par \uc2 \u8226\'20\'22 \uc2 U.S. Federal Reserve\uc2  May meeting minutes (11:30 p.m IST) \u8226\'20\'22 U.S. week to May 15 - initial jobless claim - May 25 (6:00 p.m. IST)\par \par \uc2 \u8226\'20\'22 U.S. April - PCE price index and core PCE price index - May 26 (6:00 p.m. IST) ($1 = 81.7800 Indian rupees) (Reporting by Dharamraj Dhutia; Editing by Varun H K) \par \par \uc2 Released: 