\par\par \b \uc2 India's  \highlight22\uc2 forex \highlight\uc2  reserves at comfortable level due to \uc2 RBI\uc2 , dollar - economists\b0\par\par\uc2 407 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 Mai 2023\par 10:13\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 MUMBAI, May 9 (Reuters) - India's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves are at a comfortable level currently, benefiting from the \uc2 Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2 's persistent \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the likelihood of less volatile revaluation changes, economists said.\par \par \uc2 India's \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves reached a 10-month-high of $588.8 billion in the week through April 28, recovering from a drop to $524.5 billion last October, when the rupee hit a record low against the U.S. dollar.\par \par \uc2 Since October 2022, the \uc2 RBI\uc2  has been rebuilding the reserves, taking advantage of the rupee's recovery. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bought more than $8 billion in the spot market in November and December, according to its data.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 RBI\uc2  has been buying dollars in the forward market too. Its net outstanding forward dollar purchases reached $20.4 billion in February, the latest month for which data is available, after having dwindled to $241 million in October.\par \par \uc2 Since October, "comfort on the level of reserves has improved significantly," said Gaura Sen Gupta, economist at \uc2 IDFC First Bank\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "Reserves (both spot and forwards) are now equivalent to 10.4 months of import cover, compared with about 8.9% in Oct 2022."\par \par \uc2 Moreover, said Vivek Kumar, economist at QuantEco Research, the \uc2 RBI\uc2  will continue "using reserve buildup opportunity to bolster its import cover further".\par \par \uc2 In the wake of the \uc2 RBI\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in spot and forwards, the rupee's response to the dollar's decline has been fairly muted.\par \par \uc2 While the dollar index has lost about 11.5% from its peak in September last year, the rupee has recovered about 1.5% from its record low.\par \par \uc2 And, given that the \uc2 RBI\uc2  will want the rupee "to remain ranged", the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  will continue to somewhat underperform, said Sakshi Gupta, Principal Economist, \uc2 HDFC Bank\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Besides the \uc2 RBI\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the revaluation changes due to the dollar index's decline since October and the fall in U.S. yields have been major contributors to the increase in reserves.\par \par \uc2 Now, with the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  likely to pause its rate-hike cycle and the U.S. dollar's soft outlook, there is expected to be less volatility around revaluation changes, said IDFC's Gaura.\par \par \uc2 And provides an added layer of comfort as far as the adequacy of \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves is concerned.\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Nimesh Vora; Editing by Savio D'Souza) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2023-5-9T09:13:52.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020230509ej5901h36\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
