\par\par \b \uc2 REUTERS NEXT-India to step up state spending to boost growth - finance minister\b0\par\par\uc2 597 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 November 2022\par 17:07\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 FinMin speaking ahead of February annual budget\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Last full budget before 2024 elections\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Sees "very good" \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  economy next year\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Sees rupee stabilising without much \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 (Adds comments on Russian energy imports, details of plans to boost economic growth)\par \par \uc2 By Ira Dugal and Aftab Ahmed\par \par \uc2 NEW DELHI, Nov 30 (Reuters) - India plans to use government capital spending to sustain strong economic growth, finance minister \uc2 Nirmala Sitharaman\uc2  told\u8239\'20\'2F the Reuters NEXT conference on Wednesday, forecasting a "very good" economy ahead of 2024 national elections.\par \par \uc2 The government will also place a priority on health and education, said Sitharaman, who is preparing the national budget due on Feb. 1, the last full budget before the elections.\par \par \uc2 "We would continue to push capital expenditure, and that I\u8217\'20\'19m saying even as I\u8217\'20\'19m preparing for the next budget," Sitharaman told the conference. Capital expenditure, she said, "can guarantee good growth."\par \par \uc2 As a result, India can look forward to "a very good... growing Indian economy this year and the next," she said.\par \par \uc2 India's capital spending budget for the current fiscal year is already 27% higher than the previous year.\par \par \uc2 The government's top economic advisor on Wednesday forecast economic growth for the fiscal year to March 31 at 6.8-7%.\par \par \uc2 Economists have warned, however, that growth momentum may ease in the coming months due to higher interest rates and slowing exports.\par \par \uc2 Other measures to boost growth include a possible expansion of India's main industrial incentive scheme beyond the current sectors, which include autos, electronic manufacturing and solar, Sitharaman said.\par \par \uc2 Economic reforms will continue despite the elections, she said.\par \par \uc2 IMPORTS FROM RUSSIA\par \par \uc2 Higher oil imports from Russia are helping the Indian economy and boosting consumer sentiment as cheaper energy costs partly offset rises in prices elsewhere, Sitharaman told the conference.\par \par \uc2 Affordability is "one of the very important considerations," for an import-dependant country supporting a population of over 1 billion, said Sitharaman, who said Western countries are also taking advantage of cheaper oil from Russia.\par \par \uc2 Indian imports from Russia totalled $29 billion between Feb. 24 and Nov. 20 an almost fivefold increase on the $6 billion in the same period a year ago, a source told Reuters this week.\par \par \uc2 Earlier this month, \uc2 U.S. Treasury\uc2  Secretary \uc2 Janet Yellen\uc2  said she was happy for India to continue buying Russian oil on certain conditions.\par \par \uc2 As part of India's G20 presidency, which began earlier this month, Sitharaman said India will push for a multilateral framework for climate funding and cryptocurrency .\par \par \uc2 Asked about its investigations against some Chinese companies, she said India has no plans to clamp down on investments from any specific country.\par \par \uc2 RUPEE TO STABILISE\par \par \uc2 The government is comfortable with the country's current \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves but believes that the rupee will stabilise without much \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The rupee fell below 83 against the dollar earlier in the year, before recovering in the last few weeks on the back of signals of a slower monetary tightening from the United States. On Wednesday the rupee finished the session at 81.4.\par \par \uc2 Inflation is being influenced by external factors for which measures will need to be taken, she said.\par \par \uc2 But the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  expects inflation will be "within the tolerance band by early next year or middle of next year," she added.\par \par \uc2 ($1 = 81.2750 Indian rupees) (Additional reporting by Manoj Kumar and Nikunj Ohri in New Delhi, Shilpa Jamkhandikar in Mumbai; Editing by Andrew Heavens and Conor Humphries) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2022-11-30T17:07:34.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020221130eibu03k0e\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
