\par\par \b \uc2 SocGen recommends GBP/INR carry trade, receiving 5-year OIS\b0\par\par\uc2 357 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 November 2022\par 07:14\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 MUMBAI, Nov 23 (Reuters) - \uc2 Societe Generale\uc2  recommends selling the GBP/INR, or the British pound and Indian rupee pair, due to attractive carry-to-volatility and suggests receiving longer-tenure rupee overnight index swaps (OIS) given growth risks.\par \par \uc2 The French lender pointed out the rupee offers the highest carry to volatility among Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and has remained resilient against the major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , except the U.S. dollar (USD).\par \par \uc2 "This resilience might well persist into 2023 and we believe a short GBP/INR to be a more constructive expression to own INR carry," it said in a note on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 A carry trade involves buying a higher-yielding \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  vis-a-vis a lower-yielding one. Apart from the yield difference, the expected volatility of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  is considered when making a carry trade.\par \par \uc2 Further supporting its case for the short GBP/INR trade, SocGen highlighted that the upside on USD/INR is effectively capped by the \uc2 Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 forex interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the United Kingdom is expected to post the weakest growth among G10 economies in 2023, as well as the second-biggest current account deficit, SocGen said.\par \par \uc2 The short GBP/INR trade offers a carry of around +0.33% per month, according to the lender. It recommends a stop loss of 102 and a profit target of 84 for an entry point of 96.6 for the trade.\par \par \uc2 On the rate side, SocGen suggests receiving the 5-year offshore OIS at around 6.5%, with a target of 5% and a stop loss of 7.25%, mainly due to growth concerns.\par \par \uc2 "Growth-related risks should be incrementally priced into the rates market in 2023," it said, adding that certain members of the \uc2 Indian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's Monetary Policy Committee have already raised concerns about over-tightening.\par \par \uc2 SocGen expects India's growth to slow from 6.9% this fiscal to 6.5% next fiscal and inflation to progressively ease below the \uc2 RBI\uc2 's upper threshold of 6% in 2023. (Reporting by Nimesh Vora; Editing by Savio D'Souza) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2022-11-23T07:14:21.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020221123eibn00ojg\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
