\par\par \b \uc2 INDIA RUPEE-Rupee posts tenth monthly decline on weak Asian peers, oil price worries\b0\par\par\uc2 356 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 31 Oktober 2022\par 11:28\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 MUMBAI, Oct 31 (Reuters) - The Indian rupee chalked up its tenth straight monthly fall after slipping versus the dollar on Monday, on concerns over oil prices and in line with losses in most other Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The rupee last traded at 82.78 per U.S. dollar on Monday, compared with 82.47 in the previous session.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  depreciated 1.8% in October. The last time it had a positive month against the dollar was in December 2021.\par \par \uc2 This is rupee's longest monthly losing run since 1985. The string of monthly losses has put the rupee on course for its worst annual performance in almost a decade.\par \par \uc2 "Fundamental issues such as high current account deficit and persistent inflation remain impediments (for the rupee)," Srinivas Puni, managing director at QuantArt Market Solutions, said.\par \par \uc2 Among the rupee's Asian peers, the offshore Chinese yuan and the Indonesian rupiah dropped about 2.4% each in October, while the Malaysian ringgit fell 2% and the Thai baht 0.6%. The Korean won outperformed, inching up.\par \par \uc2 The prospect of more \uc2 U.S. Federal Reserve\uc2  rate hikes alongside worries over the Chinese economic outlook impacted the demand for Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Expectations of the Fed slowing its pace of rate hikes have increased in recent days, though the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is poised to deliver yet another super-sized 75 basis point rate hike this week.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, Brent crude rose more than 8% this month, set to halt a four-month losing run, after OPEC+ key agreed to production cuts.\par \par \uc2 The rupee's losses this month would have been deeper had it not been for the \uc2 Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold dollars to boost the rupee, likely via the forward market, traders said.\par \par \uc2 RBI\uc2 's forward dollar sales and higher Treasury yields pushed rupee forward premiums lower. The 1-year USD/INR implied forward rate dropped to near 2.40% from 2.81% in September.\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Nimesh Vora; Editing by Dhanya Thoppil) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2022-10-31T11:28:40.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020221031eiav01yg7\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
