\par\par \b \uc2 Indian bond yields seen rising, rupee may test record lows\b0\par\par\uc2 396 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 Oktober 2022\par 03:30\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 MUMBAI, Oct 17 (Reuters) - The Indian rupee may edge towards record lows but will get some support from a less hawkish-than-expected tone in minutes of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's latest policy meeting, while government bond yields are expected to rise, analysts said.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Reserve Bank of India\uc2 's monetary policy committee (MPC) may become more data-dependant in deciding on the key interest rate with inflation expected to start easing, minutes of the latest meeting suggested on Friday.\par \par \uc2 "We could see a narrow trading range of 7.40%-7.50% on the 10-year bond next week as we do not have any major scheduled events," a trader with a private bank said.\par \par \uc2 The benchmark Indian government bond yield ended at 7.4696% on Friday, 5 basis points (bps) above its previous close. The yield rose 1 basis point on the week, after adding an aggregate 29 bps over the last four weeks.\par \par \uc2 Market participants, however, do not expect the benchmark bond yield to break 7.50% in a major way.\par \par \uc2 Traders said with MPC members divided on the future trajectory on rate hikes, there was some relief buying expected.\par \par \uc2 India's annual retail inflation accelerated to a five-month high of 7.41% in September, its ninth straight reading above the \uc2 RBI\uc2 's target band of 2-6% but wholesale price inflation fell to an 18-month low, separate data last week showed.\par \par \uc2 The fall in wholesale prices has raised expectations of less aggressive monetary tightening going ahead.\par \par \uc2 The rupee is likely to track movements in the dollar and crude oil prices next week. It is expected to hover around record lows but traders will watch out for dollar selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The local unit hit a life low of 82.6825 at the start of last week, prompting the \uc2 Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  to step in with dollar sales.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 RBI\b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  heavily on Monday and Tuesday, traders said, and there was likely some sporadic \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on other days.\par \par \uc2 "Markets are expected to remain jittery. Any dip in spot USD/INR should be bought with rupee likely to move to 83 to 83.50," said Amit Pabari, managing director at CR \b \highlight22\uc2 Forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  Advisors. (Writing by Swati Bhat; Editing by Anil D'Silva) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2022-10-17T02:30:00.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020221017eiah00691\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw103\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal1545\pichgoal600
