\par\par \uc2 CE Noticias Financieras English\par \b \uc2 Global international reserves lose one trillion dollars, a record reduction\b0\par\par\uc2 445 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 Oktober 2022\par \uc2 CE NoticiasFinancieras\par \uc2 NFINCE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 Content Engine LLC \par \par \uc2 Oct. 13 (Bloomberg) -- Global \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves are falling at the fastest pace on record as central banks from India to the Czech Republic step in to support their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Reserves have declined by about $1 trillion, or 7.8%, to $12 trillion this year, the biggest drop since \uc2 Bloomberg\uc2  began compiling the data in 2003.\par \par \uc2 Part of the drop is simply due to changes in valuation. After the dollar hit two-decade highs against other reserve \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , such as the euro and the yen, this reduced the dollar value of holdings in those \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . But the decline in reserves also reflects the stress in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, which is forcing a growing number of central banks to dip into their "vaults" to defend against depreciation.\par \par \uc2 India's reserves, for example, fell $96 billion so far this year to $538 billion. The country's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said changes in asset valuation accounted for 67% of the decline in reserves during the fiscal year from April, implying that the rest came from \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to shore up the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The rupee has lost about 9% against the dollar this year and hit a record low last month.\par \par \uc2 Japan spent about $20 billion in September to stem the yen's slide, in its first \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  since 1998. That would account for about 19% of the loss in value of reserves this year. A \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the Czech Republic caused the country's reserves to fall 19% since February.\par \par \uc2 "This is all part of the catalog of symptoms," \uc2 Axel Merk\uc2 , chief investment officer at \uc2 Merk Investments\uc2 , said of the declining reserves. "Cracks are appearing and those red flags [red flags] will come at an increasing rate."\par \par \uc2 While the magnitude of the drop is extraordinary, the practice of using reserves to defend \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  is nothing new. Central banks buy dollars and accumulate reserves to slow \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  appreciation when foreign capital arrives en masse. In bad times, they reduce reserves to soften the blow of capital flight.\par \par \uc2 "Some countries, especially in Asia, can go both ways, softening weakness and pockets of strength," said \uc2 Alan Ruskin\uc2 , chief international strategist at \uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Most central banks still have enough firepower to maintain \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 , if they so choose. \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves in \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  are still 49% higher than 2017 levels, and enough to pay for nine months of imports.\par \par \uc2 But for others, they are rapidly depleting. After declining 42% this year, Pakistan's $14 billion in reserves are not enough to cover three months of imports, according to what data compiled by \uc2 Bloomberg\uc2  show.\par \par \uc2 Dokument NFINCE0020221013eiad008nd\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
