\par\par \b \uc2 INDIA RUPEE-Rupee dips as Fed rate hike bets, risk aversion prop dollar\b0\par\par\uc2 307 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 Oktober 2022\par 07:33\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 MUMBAI, Oct 11 (Reuters) - The Indian rupee traded a bit weaker on Tuesday, as geopolitical tensions and expectations of large interest rate hikes by the \uc2 U.S. Federal Reserve\uc2  boosted the dollar, with a spike in yields burnishing the greenback further.\par \par \uc2 The partially convertible rupee eased 0.1% to 82.40 by 0505 GMT. It had hit a record low of 82.6825 in the previous session, but recouped all the losses by close on likely \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \uc2 Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2 , traders pointed out.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is likely to have stepped in on Tuesday, although not "as aggressively," which kept the rupee from declining as much as its Asian counterparts, two traders said.\par \par \uc2 Oil prices retracing to under $96 per barrel has also helped the rupee a bit, one trader said, adding that the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  could weaken to 83 and beyond around the Fed meeting next month.\par \par \uc2 The dollar index jumped to 113.4 as worries about rising interest rates and geopolitical tensions unsettled investors after a Russian missile attack on Ukraine killed civilians and knocked out power and heat in some cites.\par \par \uc2 Asian stocks marked heavy losses, while Indian equities lost 0.3%.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the 10-year \uc2 U.S. Treasury\uc2  yield jumped to 4% as a rout in the UK gilts market created turbulence amid fears pension funds were being forced into fire sales of assets due to rising yields.\par \par \uc2 In the domestic market, investors are now looking to India's retail inflation figures due on Wednesday, which is likely to have accelerated to a five-month high of 7.30% in September due to surging food prices, a Reuters poll showed. (Reporting by Anushka Trivedi in Mumbai; Editing by Dhanya Ann Thoppil) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2022-10-11T06:33:00.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020221011eiab00n5d\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
