\par\par \b \uc2 EMERGING MARKETS-Asian  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 , stocks tumble on bets of more U.S. rate hikes\b0\par\par\uc2 663 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 Oktober 2022\par 10:12\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par         * \par       Dollar higher after strong U.S. jobs data\par     \par \par         * \par       Thai baht plummets 1.4%\par     \par \par         * \par       Philippine peso supported by c.bank \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par     \par \par         * \par       Stocks suffer on higher yields\par     \par \par         * \par       \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 India\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  c.bank likely sold dollars - sources\par     \par \par   \par     By Harshita Swaminathan\par        Oct 10 (Reuters) - Most Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  weakened\par against dollar on Monday, as strong U.S. jobs data raised the\par chances of further \f28 \uc2 \f2 Federal Reserve\f28 \uc2 \f2  interest rate hikes, casting\par a cloud on global growth. \par     Unemployment in September fell further, data from the U.S.\par showed, which points to a tight labour market, while job growth\par slowed moderately. \par     "A surprising drop in the unemployment rate and another\par solid wage growth have boosted market expectations for a fourth\par 75bp rise at the November 1-2 FOMC (Federal Open Market\par Committee) meeting," said Gao Qi, a \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at\par Scotiabank. \par     The Thai baht, which he said was a "high-beta" \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ,\par tumbled 1.4% to trade at 37.9 to the dollar. The Indonesian\par rupiah fell 0.3%, and the Singapore dollar\par weakened 0.1%. \par         The greeenback further strengthened after the jobs data,\par with the dollar index rising 0.5% late on Friday, and\par adding another 0.1% Monday. The index was last seen at 112.861. \par   \par         However, the Philippine peso held up against the\par strong dollar, rising 0.1%, supported by the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par governor's statements late on Friday, when he said the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bangko\par Sentral ng Pilipinas\f28 \uc2 \f2  was active in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market and selling\par strategically. \par     Malaysia's prime minister said the parliament was dissolved,\par with the dates of a snap election yet to be decided. The ringgit\par  did not trade on account of a public holiday.\par  \par     The prospect of further U.S. rate hikes also sent the\par country's benchmark yield 6.1 basis points (bps)\par higher to 3.885% on Friday. Asian yields followed suit, with\par Indonesia's 10-year bond yield up 4.6 bps at 7.288%,\par while the Singaporean yield pushing 4.7 bps higher\par to 3.457%. \par     Meanwhile, all major Asian stock markets traded in the red,\par with China leading losses, after shedding 1.7%. Markets\par in the Philippines also tumbled 1.7%, while those in\par Singapore fell 1.3%. \par      "Returns from safe assets keep rising, making it harder for\par equities to maintain their attractiveness," analysts at DBS\par said. \par     Chinese technology stocks fell sharply after U.S. President\par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Joe Biden\f28 \uc2 \f2 's administration published a sweeping set of export\par controls on Friday, including a measure to cut China off from\par certain semiconductor chips made anywhere in the world with U.S.\par equipment. \par     Markets in China reopened after a weeklong holiday, with\par China's onshore yuan trading about 0.1% higher. \par     Data showed China's services activity shrank in September\par due to COVID-19 restrictions, the first contraction in four\par months. \par     The Indian rupee hit a record low of 82.6825 to the\par dollar before cutting losses after the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  stepped in \par and sold dollars, sources told Reuters. The rupee last traded at\par 82.39 to the dollar. \par     Markets in Malaysia, South Korea and Taiwan did not trade on\par account of public holidays. Japan's stock market was closed, but\par the yen traded. \par HIGHLIGHTS:\par     ** Malaysia on Friday warned of an economic slowdown while\par unveiling a smaller budget for the upcoming year \par     ** India fuel demand slips to 10-month low in September\par  \par   Asia stock indexes and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  at                          \par   0711 GMT                                                \par  COUNTRY      \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  RIC         \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  YTD    INDEX  STOCKS  STOCKS\par                           DAILY       %            DAILY   YTD %\par                               %                        %  \par  Japan                    +0.05  -20.78             -     -5.82\par  China                    +0.08  -10.68            -1.69  -18.31\par  India                    -0.08   -9.77            -0.63   -0.86\par  Indonesia                -0.39   -6.92            -0.68    6.04\par  Philippines              +0.07  -13.57            -1.68  -18.11\par  Singapore                -0.13   -5.98            -1.17   -0.47\par  Thailand                 -1.33  -11.81            -0.96   -5.61\par     \par  (Reporting by Harshita Swaminathan, Additional reporting by\par Jaskiran Singh)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Released: 2022-10-10T09:12:21.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020221010eiaa011kt\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
