\par\par \b \uc2 India Needs More Than Reserves to Fight the Fed -- WSJ\b0\par\par\uc2 By Megha Mandavia \par \uc2 503 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 September 2022\par 14:12\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2022, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 Throwing money at the problem might not work for India anymore. In its battle against a strengthening dollar, the \uc2 Indian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  might have to accept there is little it can do to control the slide of the Indian rupee, which is now trading near a record low of around 82 to the dollar.\par \par \uc2 As a result of aggressive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \uc2 Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  in recent months, \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves had dipped to $551 billion by early September, down from $633.6 billion at the end of 2021.\par \par \uc2 Reserves are now equal to 8.4 months of imports, notes \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2 . That figure was 13 months at the end of 2021, according to the \uc2 RBI\uc2 . \uc2 Abheek Barua\uc2 , chief economist at \uc2 HDFC Bank\uc2 , thinks the \uc2 RBI\uc2  will have to find more ways to bolster reserves if they slip below $500 billion.\par \par \uc2 With \b \highlight22\uc2 forex interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  eating up reserves fast and no clear end to the dollar rally in sight, the \uc2 RBI\uc2  at the very least needs to be more judicious in deploying its resources -- and probably ultimately allow the Indian rupee to find its own value. One thing it can do is aggressively use macroprudential and capital-account reforms to boost inflows, but even that is likely to lean against the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's slide, rather than fully break it.\par \par \uc2 Since India depends heavily on imported energy, the rupee has been hard hit this year: down 10% against the dollar versus 9% for the MSCI Emerging Markets \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  Index, according to \uc2 FactSet\uc2 . India imports more than 80% of the oil it consumes, and inflation has been above the upper limit of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's target range -- 6% -- for eight consecutive months.\par \par \uc2 Now, just when oil prices were beginning to fall in dollar terms, more \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  weakness looks likely to take away that breathing room. The \uc2 RBI\uc2  has already raised benchmark interest rates by 1.4 percentage points this year and will probably add another half point raise this Friday.\par \par \uc2 One thing the \uc2 RBI\uc2  can do, besides raising its own policy rates, is make it easier for foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to come in -- and make it more attractive to invest in India in general. In July, the \uc2 RBI\uc2  relaxed debt-market rules for foreign portfolio investors and allowed commercial banks to raise interest rates on nonresidents' foreign-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  deposits. On the other hand, Delhi's increasingly aggressive regulatory stance toward American tech firms and the recent performance of many Indian IPOs might give some investors pause.\par \par \uc2 India's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  isn't in an enviable position right now. Attracting more dollars would help, but if the Fed stays on its current path and pushes the U.S. into recession, it might be tough for India to avoid much higher rates and a steep slowdown in growth, too.\par \par \uc2 Write to Megha Mandavia at megha.mandavia@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 September 29, 2022 08:12 ET (12:12 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020220929ei9t0026p\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
