\par\par \b \uc2 GLOBAL MARKETS-Sterling slippery, stocks stalling as \uc2 BoE\uc2  boost fades\b0\par\par\uc2 663 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 September 2022\par 08:10\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Sterling drops 1% in Asia, below $1.08\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Stock bounce runs out of puff; MSCI AxJ up just 0.5%\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Truss, \uc2 BoE\uc2  speakers on the radar\par \par \uc2 By Tom Westbrook\par \par \uc2 SYDNEY, Sept 29 (Reuters) - Asian share markets rose on Thursday after Britain's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  launched an emergency bond buying programme to stabilise a furious sell-off in gilts, though trade was skittish and sterling remained under pressure.\par \par \uc2 The Bank of England\uc2  said it will buy as much as \u163\'00\'A35 billion ($5.4 billion) a day of long-dated government bonds until Oct. 14. It spent about a billion pounds on Wednesday and 30-year gilt yields fell 105 basis points (bps), the biggest drop ever according to Refinitiv records stretching back to 1992.\par \par \uc2 The move buoyed sterling and offered some salve to a fractious mood in markets, but by mid afternoon in Tokyo the pound was struggling for support and down 1% at $1.0776.\par \par \uc2 MSCI's broadest index of Asia-Pacific shares outside Japan was up 0.5% - more muted than a 2% surge on Wall Street overnight. Japan's Nikkei rose 0.9%.\par \par \uc2 S&P 500 futures fell 0.2%. European futures rose 0.6% and FTSE futures lifted 0.3%.\par \par \uc2 "It's all a bit of a mess," said ANZ economist Finn Robinson.\par \par \uc2 "How long the calm and fresh optimism lasts remains to be seen. For one, this re-stimulation will lift, not quell UK inflation, and that's bad for bonds and sterling."\par \par \uc2 The fallout from unfunded tax cuts announced in Britain last week has reverberated across financial markets after setting off a collapse in British asset prices. The \uc2 BoE\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  followed steps in Korea, \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Indonesia to stabilise their financial markets this week as the U.S. dollar rallied broadly.\par \par \uc2 China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said stabilising the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market is the top priority, as the yuan hangs around its lowest levels since the financial crisis.\par \par \uc2 Later on Thursday, investors' focus will be on three \uc2 Bank of England\uc2  speakers, media appearances from British Prime Minister \uc2 Liz Truss\uc2 , German inflation data and Federal Reserve speakers.\par \par \uc2 "Note that nothing has fundamentally changed other than a circuit breaker provided by BOE," said DBS' rates strategist \uc2 Eugene Leow\uc2  in Singapore.\par \par \uc2 STRONG DOLLAR\par \par \uc2 U.S. Treasuries rebounded in sympathy with gilts on Wednesday, and benchmark 10-year yields fell more than 20 bps, before creeping up to 3.818% in Asia.\par \par \uc2 A drop in the dollar was also unwinding. The U.S. dollar index had its worst session in 2-1/2 years on Wednesday, recoiling from record highs. But the greenback found buyers all day in Asia, grinding the index up by 0.5% to 113.56.\par \par \uc2 The euro fell about 0.7% to $0.9665. The Australian dollar fell 0.8% to $0.6470. The kiwi fell 1% to $0.5674.\par \par \uc2 Russia is about to annex a swath of Ukraine, a step to extending its nuclear umbrella over them. The EU suspects sabotage of gas pipes beneath the Baltic Sea.\par \par \uc2 "Concerns over growth in China, the euro zone and various other economies have been underpinning demand for the dollar for months," said Rabobank \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist \uc2 Jane Foley\uc2 , who has a target for $0.95 on the euro and sees a risk it goes even lower.\par \par \uc2 "Given that risk appetite has been on the back foot for some time, the ructions in the UK market this week only served to undermine investor confidence further."\par \par \uc2 The dollar's pullback helped oil and gold make gains through Wednesday, which were slipping slightly in Asia. Brent crude futures fell 0.8% to $88.62 a barrel. Spot gold fell 1% to $1,642 an ounce.\par \par \uc2 Into this maelstrom Porsche is listing, with books closing at a price of 82.50 euros. Trade begins around 0715 GMT. ($1 = 0.9252 pounds)\par \par \uc2 (Editing by Shri Navaratnam and Kim Coghill) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2022-9-29T07:10:07.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020220929ei9t00yez\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
