\par\par \b \uc2 INDIA RUPEE-Rupee seen at record low after Fed signals more aggressive rate hikes\b0\par\par\uc2 517 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 September 2022\par 04:31\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 MUMBAI, Sept 22 (Reuters) - The Indian rupee is seen hitting a record low against the dollar on Thursday after the \uc2 U.S. Federal Reserve\uc2  hiked rates by 75 basis points on Wednesday and indicated more large rate hikes.\par \par \uc2 The rupee is expected to open around 80.25-80.30 per U.S. dollar in early trades, down from 79.9750 in the previous session. The previous record low for the local unit was 80.12 reached late in August.\par \par \uc2 The Fed's interest rate projections were "way more hawkish than expected" and the "damage we are seeing on Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  should see rupee move comfortably below 80 at open", a trader at a Mumbai-based bank said.\par \par \uc2 "It is quiet clear that what happens after the open will depend upon the \uc2 RBI\uc2  (\uc2 Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2 )," the trader said.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 RBI\uc2  had swiftly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  when rupee dropped below 80, according to traders.\par \par \uc2 Earlier this month, the \uc2 RBI\uc2  governor \uc2 Shaktikanta Das\uc2  said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  aims to anchor expectations around the depreciating rupee and will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prevent an overshoot.\par \par \uc2 The Fed on Wednesday raised rates by 75 basis points to 3%-3.25%, an outcome that was widely expected by economists. But, what took the markets by surprise was the path policymakers see for rates from here, the so-called dot plot.\par \par \uc2 The median dot now show a funds rate midpoint of 4.375% by the end of this year. This would imply cumulative hikes of 125 basis points over the next two meetings in 2022 in November and December.\par \par \uc2 ING Bank\uc2  in a note pointed out that the year-end Fed funds projection of 4.4% is above the 4.2% rate implied by futures contracts before the Fed policy outcome.\par \par \uc2 "It is important to note that there is a strong clustering within the dot-plots, showing all Fed members are on board with this more hawkish narrative," ING said.\par \par \uc2 The dollar index surged to a new twenty-year high, while the 2-year Treasury yield climbed above 4% and the yield curve inverted further.\par \par \uc2 U.S. equities dropped and futures indicated more losses for the S&P 500 Index. Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and equities tumbled. The offshore Chinese yuan dropped below 7.10 to the dollar.\par \par \uc2 KEY INDICATORS: ** One-month non-deliverable rupee forward at 80.48; onshore one-month forward premium at 21 paise ** USD/INR NSE September futures closed on Wednesday at 80.0050 ** USD/INR forward premium for end current month at 3.5 paise ** Dollar index climbs to 111.69 ** Brent crude futures at $90.3 per barrel ** Ten-year U.S. note yield at 3.56% ** SGX Nifty nearest-month futures down 0.8% at 17,579 ** As per NSDL data, foreign investors bought a net $226.4mln worth of Indian shares on Sep. 20\par \par \uc2 ** NSDL data shows foreign investors sold a net $31.1mln worth of Indian bonds on Sep. 20 (Reporting by Nimesh Vora; Editing by Neha Arora) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2022-9-22T03:31:18.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020220922ei9m009ey\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
