\par\par \b \uc2 Indian rupee gains as less-hawkish Powell calms strong rate-hike fears\b0\par\par\uc2 376 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 Juli 2022\par 08:44\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 MUMBAI, July 28 (Reuters) - The Indian rupee gained on Thursday, tracking strength in most other Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and equities, while bond yields eased after \uc2 U.S. Federal Reserve\uc2  Chair \uc2 Jerome Powell\uc2  eased trader concerns over continued aggressive monetary tightening.\par \par \uc2 Powell sounded suitably hawkish on curbing inflation in his news conference, but also dropped guidance on the size of the next rate hike and noted that "at some point" it would be appropriate to slow down.\par \par \uc2 U.S. Treasury\uc2  yields edged lower as bonds rallied after the Fed raised rates by 75 bps, in line with market expectations.\par \par \uc2 India's benchmark 10-year bond yield was trading at 7.33% by 0640 GMT, down 1 basis point on the day, while the partially convertible rupee was trading at 79.75/76 per dollar, compared with its close of 79.8975 on Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 The dollar dropped to a three-week low versus the yen, with the dollar index down 0.3%.\par \par \uc2 Traders said focus will soon shift to monetary policy committee meeting by the \uc2 Reserve Bank of India (RBI) \uc2 due next week. Though the broad consensus was for a 50-bps rate hike, views are likely to be tempered after Fed's decision.\par \par \uc2 "We now expect the RBI MPC to raise policy repo rate by 35 bps on Aug. 5 and change stance to calibrated tightening," economists at \uc2 BoFA Securities\uc2  said in a note.\par \par \uc2 Investors also expect the rupee to be supported by \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  if there is any depreciation pressure on the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the near term.\par \par \uc2 A senior source aware of \uc2 RBI\uc2 's decision-making recently told Reuters that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  could spend up to $100 billion to protect runaway falls in the rupee.\par \par \uc2 "\uc2 RBI\uc2  might not have used more than $25 billion-$30 billion in defending rupee, given the increasing penchant of the regulator to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in NDF/Future markets," \uc2 State Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  said in a recent note.\par \par \uc2 "Rest of the decline (in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves) might be purely because of valuation. Enough support for rupee is thus still available," the lender added.\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Swati Bhat; Editing by Sherry Jacob-Phillips) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2022-7-28T07:44:12.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020220728ei7s016o5\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
