\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 2-India c.bank has zero tolerance for rupee volatility - Governor Das\b0\par\par\uc2 431 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 Juli 2022\par 09:21\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 (Updates with quotes, details)\par \par \uc2 By Nupur Anand and Swati Bhat\par \par \uc2 MUMBAI, July 22 (Reuters) - \uc2 India's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has zero tolerance for volatile and bumpy movements in the rupee and will continue to engage with the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to ensure the rupee finds its appropriate level, its chief \uc2 Shaktikanta Das\uc2  said on Friday.\par \par \uc2 The rupee has been hitting a series of record lows in recent weeks and broke past the psychological 80 per dollar mark earlier this week, prompting heavy dollar selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from the \uc2 Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  (RBI)\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "We have no particular level of rupee in mind but we would like to ensure orderly evolution," Das said at a banking conference in Mumbai organised by the \uc2 Bank of Baroda\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "We have zero tolerance for high volatility and bumpy movements."\par \par \uc2 Das said the rupee's movements have been relatively smooth and orderly due to the \uc2 RBI\uc2 's actions in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. By ensuring there are no sudden and volatile shifts, the \uc2 RBI\uc2  was keeping expectations anchored and the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market functioning in a stable and liquid manner, he added.\par \par \uc2 "We will continue to engage with the \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market and ensure that rupee finds its level in line with its fundamentals".\par \par \uc2 He said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been supplying dollars since there is a genuine shortage of dollars in the market.\par \par \uc2 On Friday, the rupee was last trading at 79.90/91 to a dollar, compared to its Thursday's close of 79.9450.\par \par \uc2 Das also said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has built its \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves for situations like these, noting that India has a substantial amount of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves to handle the current crisis and there is no need to panic.\par \par \uc2 More broadly, the governor said India's economy remains well placed and all high-frequency indicators have been pointing towards a steady revival.\par \par \uc2 However, on inflation he said that while it appears to have peaked, the situation remains volatile and the \uc2 RBI\uc2  was constantly reviewing it.\par \par \uc2 India's annual consumer inflation remained above the 7% mark and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's 2%-6% tolerance band for the sixth month in a row in June but has come down from an eight-year peak of 7.79% seen in April.\par \par \uc2 Das said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's goal is and will continue to be to ensure a soft landing for the economy. (Reporting by Nupur Anand and Swati Bhat; Editing by Rashmi Aich and Sonali Desai) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2022-7-22T08:21:41.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020220722ei7m00q8j\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
