\par\par \b \uc2 BREAKINGVIEWS-India\u8217\'20\'19s crisis buffers are part optical illusion\b0\par\par\uc2 598 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 Juli 2022\par 06:52\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 (The author is a Reuters Breakingviews columnist. The opinions expressed are her own. Refiles to add graphic.)\par \par \uc2 By Una Galani\par \par \uc2 MUMBAI, July 4 (Reuters Breakingviews) - A bear market magnifies weaknesses. In India, imported inflation caused by the war in Ukraine and aggravated by the \uc2 U.S. Federal Reserve\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s aggressive rate hikes are depressing the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and reviving the country\u8217\'20\'19s terrible twin deficits \u8211\'20\'13 the current account and the budget. The government\u8217\'20\'19s defence buffers, key to maintaining foreign investors\u8217\'20\'19 confidence and propping up the exchange rate, are thinner than they look.\par \par \uc2 Rocketing costs of imported food and energy will nearly triple the current account shortfall to 3.2% of GDP by March, Emkay Global estimates. Meanwhile, the fiscal shortfall remains high at over 6% of output, limiting room for further spending. The rupee has softened 6% to trade around 79 per dollar in 2022, a new record.\par \par \uc2 Fiscal and monetary policy have necessarily diverged. The government has extended a free food scheme until September and slashed taxes on fuel to ease living costs, but the finance ministry\u8217\'20\'19s expenditure department warns against further generosity. To restrain consumer price inflation that touched 7% in June, \uc2 Reserve Bank of India\uc2  Governor \uc2 Shaktikanta Das\uc2  hiked benchmark interest rates. Foreign funds that own two fifths of India\u8217\'20\'19s equity market are nonetheless fleeing, accelerating the weakening of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Keeping up with the Fed also risks stifling India\u8217\'20\'19s post-pandemic recovery. Gross bank credit grew 12.1% in May, which is good news but coming off a low base.\par \par \uc2 In this context, the rupee has unsurprisingly continued to weaken. New Delhi is suddenly scrambling to slow outflows, hiking taxes on gold imports and on petroleum exports, and Das has run down \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves to stem the rout: the stock has fallen to $593 billion in June, a decrease of nearly $50 billion since September. That remainder might sound intimidating, but it is only equivalent to 11 months of import cover, close to the pre-Covid five-year average, per \uc2 Citi\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 India\u8217\'20\'19s \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  problem is not unique, and the economy is in better condition compared to other developing countries, in part thanks to its push to clean up bad debt before the Ukraine crisis hit. Policymakers had managed to kill off an earlier so-called twin balance sheet problem, of which the fiscal deficit was one half, by slashing bad corporate debt to a six-year low as of March. But if unilateral \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to defend the rupee doesn\u8217\'20\'19t work, \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  will have wasted precious reserves and damaged the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s credibility.\par \par \uc2 Follow @ugalani {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/ugalani"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 here}}}\uc2 on Twitter\par \par \uc2 CONTEXT NEWS India raised taxes on gold imports, petroleum product exports and crude oil production as of July 1. Import taxes on gold were lifted to 12.5% from 7.5%. The country is the world\u8217\'20\'19s second biggest consumer of the yellow metal and meets most of its demand from overseas purchases. The measures are intended to support the exchange rate and slow energy price inflation.\par \par \uc2 The rupee is trading around 79 per dollar, weakening 6% since the start of the calendar year. That compares to a 5% softening in the Indonesian rupiah and 7% by the Thai baht.\par \par \uc2 Consumer prices including food and fuel will rise 6.7% in the full year to March, the \uc2 Reserve Bank of India\uc2  estimates. India targets 4% inflation with a tolerance band of plus or minus 2 percentage points.\par \par \uc2 (Editing by Pete Sweeney and Thomas Shum)\par \par \uc2 Released: 2022-7-7T04:52:02.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020220704ei7400ac9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
