1, 50% of the 2020-2021 rise Scant reason to fight the U.S. dollar uptrend The U.S. dollar's short-lived dive early in Asia Monday confirms that the uptrend remains resilient, supporting further gains this week. As U.S. inflation continues to rise, the economy remains strong, but there are divided views on its future trajectory. \uc2 BofA\uc2  believes aggressive \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  tightening will cause a "recession shock". Meanwhile, BlackRock does not expect rising Treasury yields to trigger an imminent U.S. recession despite last week's brief yield curve inversion. \uc2 Cleveland Fed\uc2  president \uc2 Loretta Mester\uc2  said Sunday that inflation will remain high this year and next even as the Fed moves steadily to lower the pace of price increases. The Fed is likely to be aggressive in the short term, which will continue to provide underlying U.S. dollar support. The Ukraine conflict is also supportive of the dollar, as the economic fallout will hit Europe and the euro, the largest component of the index, much harder economically than the U.S. The dollar's safe-haven status continues to attract inflows. Technically 5, 10 and 21 daily and weekly moving averages head higher, daily momentum studies are mixed, but 21-day Bollinger bands expand. This is a positive trending setup. The next significant resistance is at the 100.56 May 2020 high, while a close below the 98.86 rising 21-day moving average is needed to undermine the topside bias. INR may hold its ground as \uc2 RBI\uc2  turns hawkish The Indian rupee could hold its ground versus the strengthening U.S. dollar and even gain slightly versus Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  peers on the \uc2 Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2 's baby steps towards policy normalisation and \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , as well as expectations of foreign portfolio inflows. The \uc2 RBI\uc2  on Friday held the repo rate at 4% and maintained its accommodative stance but introduced a new tool which sets a higher floor for the interest rate corridor, signalling a nuanced move away from its ultra-loose monetary policy. The \uc2 RBI\uc2  prioritised inflation above growth for the first time in three years and raised its inflation forecast for the current fiscal year by 120 basis point to 5.7%, to better reflect actual price pressures. This should help restore its inflation-fighting credibility and support the INR. India's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves fell by $11.17 billion in the week ended April 1 - the steepest fall in two years - revealing the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's determined \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stem INR volatility. The higher possibility of state-run \uc2 Life Insurance Corp\uc2 's record IPO going ahead in April and a merger proposal between two domestic financial giants will raise expectations of foreign portfolio inflows, which should support the INR. With USD/INR likely to consolidate in 75.50-76.50 range within a broader 75.00-77.00 band, trading the range looks prudent. Week Ahead-French election, \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  meetings in focus The week ahead is shortened by holidays but holds plenty of potential market-moving events, including three \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  decisions, and developments in the French presidential election. Early results from the first round of voting Sunday show incumbent \uc2 Emmanuel Macron\uc2  with a slim lead over far-right challenger \uc2 Marine Le Pen\uc2 . This lifted the euro early Monday, but it will face fresh pressure if \uc2 Le Pen\uc2 's odds appear to improve towards the April 24 runoff vote. The Russia-Ukraine crisis continues, with chances of a swift diplomatic solution fading, though a planned meeting between Austria's leader and the Russian president has provided a glimmer of hope. Markets will also be monitoring decisions from the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , \uc2 Reserve Bank of New Zealand\uc2 , and \uc2 Bank of Canada\uc2  this week. The \uc2 ECB\uc2  faces the difficult task of balancing rampant inflation expectations and supporting growth, as the euro zone economic outlook darkens. President \uc2 Christine Lagarde\uc2  may lean hawkish at the press conference and leave the door open for a September hike. The latest Reuters poll reveals 15 of 21 analysts believe the \uc2 RBNZ\uc2  will hike rates by 25 basis points to 1.25% on Wednesday with six predicting a 50-basis-point increase; it will be a close call. The \uc2 BOC\uc2  will likely raise rates by 50 basis points on Wednesday due to soaring inflation, its second consecutive hike. U.S. earnings season kicks off with major banks, including \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2 , \uc2 Citigroup\uc2 , \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2  and \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2 , due to report. Week Ahead-U.S. CPI to impact Federal Reserve expectations The release of U.S. consumer price data for March dominates this week's economic calendar. Core CPI is expected to rise 6.6% year-on-year; if the data meets or exceeds forecasts, it will cement expectations of an aggressive Fed tightening cycle. Other key data out of the U.S. includes retail sales, PPI, weekly jobless claims and \uc2 University of Michigan\uc2  preliminary April consumer sentiment. It will be a quiet week for euro zone data as the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  meets; German ZEW and inflation are the only releases of interest. The UK has a busy week, with February GDP, manufacturing output, trade and employment, as well as March CPI, PPI and RPI all due. China will release March trade data, following CPI and PPI on Monday. House prices and outstanding loan growth will also be on tap. In Japan, machinery orders are the only significant release. Employment data will be the key event in Australia after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  made a hawkish pivot last week and highlighted data as key to the timing of policy changes. The market is expecting an increase of 40,000 jobs in March and the unemployment to ease to 3.9% from 4.0%. New Zealand and Canada have \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  decisions but no significant data due.\par \par \uc2 To read our latest \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  news and analysis services, click on\par \par \uc2 Released: 2022-4-14T05:30:50.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020220411ei4b00jwp\par }\par \b \uc2 Zusammenfassung der Suche\b0\par \par  \trowd\trgaph15\trqc\trleft-15 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx2000\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx9072\pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\intbl\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 \uc2 Text\cell \pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\intbl\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 \li50\uc2 (foreign exchange or fx or forex or currenc*) and (interven* or operation*) and (india near10 interven*) and (rst=trtw or rst=tprw or rst=tdjw) and (central bank or ministry of finance or treasury ministry or monetary authority)\cell \pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 {  \trowd\trgaph15\trqc\trleft-15 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx2000\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx9072\row } \trowd\trgaph15\trqc\trleft-15 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx2000\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx9072\pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\intbl\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 \uc2 Datum\cell \pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\intbl\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 \li50\uc2 01/01/1995 bis 21/08/2023\cell \pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 {  \trowd\trgaph15\trqc